## Rename this file to ".ldaprc" and place it in the root of your home dir.
## Update the respective TLS file paths as needed. See the "pki" directory
## in this example directory for the PKI files mentioned below.
##
## Strictly speaking, using this .ldaprc, or a system-level ldap.conf, isn't
## truly necessary for this demo. Instead, one can add the parameters below
## as environment vars by simply prefixing "LDAP" to each parameter (e.g.:
## URI becomes LDAPURI). But since this is a demo, we'll stick with a personal
## ~/.ldaprc file just because its simpler. Run "man .ldaprc" to see the man
## page for that file.

URI        ldap://127.0.0.1:10389
SASL_MECH  EXTERNAL

## VALID client certificate/key pair
## When uncommented, running "ldapwhoami -QZZ" will return the following:
##   cn=Valid User,ou=People,o=Acme
## ... which indicates a successful TLS handshake, verification and "WhoAmI?" bind.
##
TLS_CERT /path/to/valid_user/cert.pem
TLS_KEY  /path/to/valid_user/priv.key

## EXPIRED client certificate/key pair
## When uncommented, running "ldapwhoami -QZZ" will result in the following:
##   - Server-side log event: "peer certificate has expired"
##   - Client side error: "ldap_sasl_interactive_bind: Can't contact LDAP server (-1)"
##
#TLS_CERT /path/to/expired_user/cert.pem
#TLS_KEY  /path/to/expired_user/priv.key

## REVOKED client certificate/key pair
## When uncommented, running "ldapwhoami -QZZ" will result in the following:
##   - Server-side log event: "peer certificate is revoked"
##   - Client side error: "ldap_sasl_interactive_bind: Can't contact LDAP server (-1)"
##
#TLS_CERT /path/to/revoked_user/cert.pem
#TLS_KEY  /path/to/revoked_user/priv.key

## This CA bundle contains three (3) certificates -- one (1) root and
## two (2) intermediates (for "systems" and "users").
## 
## In a real production environment, one should NOT specify the CA
## manually as we're doing with TLS_CACERT. Instead, the CA(s) should
## be added to the *system trust* via the update-ca-certificates command
## (or, on some other Linux flavors, update-ca-trust). See the man pages
## for either of these commands to learn how to add issuers to the system
## trust *properly*.
##
## The OpenLDAP library will automatically check the system trust, thus
## neither TLS_CACERT nor TLS_CACERTDIR are actually needed. But for the
## sake of portability and ease-of-use, we'll opt to use TLS_CACERT for
## this demo :)
TLS_CACERT /path/to/issuer-bundle.pem
